"""Measured H.E.R.O. level data (generated from ALE ROM captures).

Backgrounds are palette-indexed 142x160 screens, RLE+base64 encoded.
Coordinates are SCREEN pixels; each room is one static screen.

LEVELS 1 TO 6 are generated by ``tools/hero_gen/build_level.py``, which
walks the level through the game's own room-transition code, captures each
room off the real ROM and refuses to emit anything unless the capture agrees
with ``level_images/hero_rooms.py`` cell for cell. Nothing in them -
geometry, palette, miner, creatures, lanterns, liquid, magma or blastable
column - is hand-typed. Re-running the generator reproduces each block
exactly, and ``tests/games/hero/test_levelN.py`` re-checks the agreement from
the committed data alone, with no emulator.

LEVEL 5 was the last of them, on 2026-09-22, and getting there took
three fixes to the walker, all of them in ``level_images/tools/hero_extract.py``
and all measured: a room is left through an open SIDE of the screen as well as
through its floor (which is what stopped the walk at room 1 and left six of
the eight rooms coming off the recorded video); a two-cell hole is only 8 px
wide, so the descent has to sweep x a pixel at a time rather than aim at cell
edges; and parking the hero at RAM x = 0 to read a room walks him out of any
room that has a side exit. Level 5's block is therefore the first one whose
rooms 2-7 come from the ROM rather than from the video, and hero_rooms.py was
corrected from that walk in seven bands - four `?` cells and three corridors
the video read as rock and the ROM draws as magma.

LEVEL 6 was regenerated on 2026-09-22, all ten of its rooms, and it is the
first level whose creatures do not all run on one clock. Two numbers came out
of the creature census (``level_images/hero_entities.py``) rather than the
capture's own short window, because the capture cannot see them:

  * its three BOBBING SPIDERS bob on a 72-frame cycle, not the 64 the capture
    votes for. 64 is the BAT's cycle, and CHARACTERS.md and CORRECTIONS.md
    both put the spider at 70/72 against the bat's 184/64; a short window
    quantises the one onto the other and the two really are different.
  * six of its eleven creatures are STILL - the census sees them draw a
    single sprite for the whole scan - so they are now drawn as a single
    sprite (CREATURE_SPRITES). Before this the pose clock ran for them too
    and a spider the ROM never redraws swapped its legs every eight frames.

The pose HOLD is deliberately still the capture's. The census reports 1 frame
per sprite for every bobbing spider, which is the same labelling artefact
that made level 4's snakes "14 poses of 1 frame each": its connected-component
pass splits the 1 px thread off the body and rejoins it, so the mask changes
every frame while the ROM has redrawn nothing.

ANY OTHER BLOCK that still comes from the first capture pass is wrong: it read
a room by writing the room index into RAM instead of letting the ROM load the
room, which keeps room 0's corridor band for every room and is wrong in 128 of
the 166 rooms measured. See ``level_images/CORRECTIONS.md``. Each of them has
to be regenerated the way levels 1 to 6 were;
``level_images/tools/check_level.py N`` says whether a level's rooms match the
ROM today. Levels 19-20 are placeholders that alias 15-16.

Level 4 also settled a rule the rest of this file used to assume: two rooms
that draw the IDENTICAL band are NOT one physical wall. Rooms 5 and 6 both
draw ``##.......##.......................####`` with a two-cell pillar at
cells 9-10; blasting room 5's on the ROM leaves room 6's standing. SHARED_WALLS
is therefore empty for every regenerated level, and the groups still listed
for the levels that have not been rebuilt came from the superseded capture.
"""
